#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135101 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ be vectors in a vector space $V$ equipped with an inner product $\\langle \\cdot , \\cdot \\rangle.$ \n\nTwo vectors $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ are **orthogonal with respect to the inner product** $\\langle \\cdot , \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V$ if their inner product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x} , \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 0\n$$\n\nIf the two vectors are orthogonal, then we denote this by $\\mathbf{x} \\perp \\mathbf{y}.$ For instance, the vectors \n\n![](images/3d/3d118ca8f8b3ea3e1b978d7bcd0159b1774ea4e8848f39d3a2566e6931b4c2f2.svg)\n\nin $\\mathbb{R}^3$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x} , \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = x_1y_1 + 2x_2y_2 + x_3y_3\n$$\n\nare orthogonal, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =1\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (1\\cdot (-1))+0\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =2-2+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ in a vector space $V$ equipped with an inner product $\\langle \\cdot , \\cdot \\rangle$ is orthogonal to any vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$ since $\\langle \\mathbf{0}, \\mathbf{x} \\rangle = 0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "77689cf4-cbcb-5c6f-bd2f-c407e888c4cb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5089 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
